WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.499 --> 00:00:09.008
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:13.600
The main problem that we face is that our
current growth is not at a recovery speed.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.540
So what a recovery means is that you

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.410
grow at more than the rate that you
averaged over the past.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.800
In order to recover.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.040
You gotta get back to where you were, and
so

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:28.630
if you had a shock, like a big recession
that we just encountered,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.040
we have to grow at more than the long term
rate.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.680
So the long term rate in the United States
is about 3%,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.250
we usually grow at about 3% per year.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.320
unfortunately, since the recovery, we've
been growing,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.240
not at a rate above three percent, which
is what we need to get back.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.340
But actually, at a rate below three
percent.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.250
So, rather than recovering,

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.920
we're actually getting further and further
away from where we need to be.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.120
So we need to get on a better trajectory.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.440
And the best way to do that,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.100
is to increase investment, to increase
productivity.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.520
And I view that as coming primarily from
four policies.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.780
One is a lower and better more efficient
tax code.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.520
Two would be a better regulatory
structure.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.340
So a more cautious eye to regulation and
regulation that enhances economic growth.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.660
The third would be,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.730
getting the budget under control so not
growing the debt at record paces.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.730
And the fourth would be engaging in more
international trade.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.580
If you look at the US population, we're
less than 5% of the world right now.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.000
We're a much bigger part of the world
economy, but

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.740
in order to continue that status in the
world in terms of economic structure.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.020
We've gotta be using the rest of the world
as our market, not just the United States.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.150
And that means more trade.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:54.697
We've been way too reluctant to extend our
trade over the past three to four years,

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:58.149
and we need to do that more, much more
aggressively.

00:01:58.149 --> 00:02:00.885
[MUSIC]

00:02:00.885 --> 00:02:04.520
Most economists believe that we should
move to something like a consumption tax.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.240
What is consumption tax?

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.710
It means that it only taxes consumption,
it does not tax income.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.900
There are variety of technical reasons for
that.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.880
This is pretty much uncontroversial in

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.790
the sense that almost all economists think
this is the best way to go.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:21.120
The problem is making it progressive.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.920
So the easiest way to do that would be

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.300
to keep something like a current
progressive tax structure.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.070
But to allow for no taxation of
investment,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:36.820
if you do that, you're basically saying
we've taxed only consumption.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.660
A variety of ways to that again this is
technical stuff, but

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.695
the simplest way to do that is to allow
business to expense,

00:02:44.695 --> 00:02:49.006
that means deduct from their taxes all
investments that they make.

00:02:49.006 --> 00:02:56.490
That's a pro-growth strategy, and it's one
that most economists tend to like.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.774
Even if you tax the rich as much as you
can possibly tax rich,

00:02:59.774 --> 00:03:02.146
again, using the president's numbers.

00:03:02.146 --> 00:03:06.046
There's just no way we're going to even
come close to closing the gap

00:03:06.046 --> 00:03:10.700
between his projected spending and his
projected tax revenue by taxing the rich.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.750
That's already built into his numbers, and
we can't get there.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.262
So, what that implies is that if we want
to continue on this path of

00:03:19.262 --> 00:03:24.936
spending we will have to raise the average
tax for the average American by 50%.

00:03:24.936 --> 00:03:29.310
That means everybody would be paying about
50% more in taxes.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:34.210
The most likely way that that would come
about would be to impose a VAT tax,

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:39.126
which is like a, a Federal retail sales
tax on the economy.

00:03:39.126 --> 00:03:42.271
That would have negative impacts on
economic growth, and

00:03:42.271 --> 00:03:46.324
so you know, that would basically make us
look a lot more like Europe.

00:03:46.324 --> 00:03:49.293
[MUSIC]

00:03:49.293 --> 00:03:51.840
We have very large projected increases in
spending.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.740
Most of this comes from Medicare and
Medicaid.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.260
Social security is also a problem but,
social security is a problem that

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.690
most people think we can deal with more
readily.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.920
There are some pretty clean solutions to
that problem.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.630
Medicare and Medicaid are much more
problematic as we look down the road.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.890
And what we need to do is, is not cut
Medicare and

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.160
Medicaid, but we need to figure out ways
to limit the growth in

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.640
those expenditures to move to a more
efficient healthcare system.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:23.010
That probably means more incentives on the
margin for people to bear their own

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.000
costs of their own healthcare, make
decisions in a more rational way.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.950
Some of the Obama Care measures are tries,
are attempts to move in that direction.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.570
We'll see if they work.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.960
Many of the Obama Care measures are
subsidies to the middle class that

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.410
actually work in the wrong direction.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.820
So on that you know, we'll have to see if
that's cost saving.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.160
The president claims they are,

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.980
I think many are skeptical that they will
be cost saving in the long run.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:51.720
I think most people who have analyzed that
including the Congressional Budget Office.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.550
Have altered their opinions and

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.940
revised upward the cost associated with
President's Health Care Bill.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:04:59.464
But we'll see.

00:04:59.464 --> 00:05:02.533
[MUSIC]

00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:05.843
I think that the Republican view that you
should not raise taxes and

00:05:05.843 --> 00:05:08.590
that you have to focus on the spending
side.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:10.290
Comes from the view that incentives
matter, and

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:11.800
that incentives matter a whole lot.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:17.650
And if you start taxing people, eventually
the money is going to move abroad.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.650
And by the way, that's not because
Americans are going to invest elsewhere.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.240
The marginal dollar of investment in the
United States is

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.560
not coming from an American, it's coming
from a Brit or from a.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:33.380
A Dutch person or from a German who is
investing in the United States and

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.360
they can go anywhere with their money.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.850
So, as we start raising taxes, those
money,

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:42.080
those dollars are going to go elsewhere
and that's the concern.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.110
I think the Democrats have a somewhat
different view.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.930
Their view is that we can do a good bit of
redistribution.

00:05:47.932 --> 00:05:52.815
Help the poor to a significant extent
without too much effect on incentives,

00:05:52.815 --> 00:05:54.954
without too much effect on growth.

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:57.822
[MUSIC]

00:05:57.822 --> 00:06:00.910
During the recession, productivity
actually went up.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:04.310
And you'd say, how could productivity go
up during a recession?

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.430
That's weird.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:10.520
Well the reason for that is that what
happened was, although output fell,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:14.810
demand for products, fell employment fell
by even more.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:19.360
So what that means is that the remaining
workers actually had to work harder.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.840
They had to make up for their colleagues
who were laid off, or

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.650
the people who were not rehired during the
recession.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.552
And in fact that happened, and Catherine
Shaw,

00:06:28.552 --> 00:06:30.821
one of my colleagues at the business
school.

00:06:30.821 --> 00:06:34.063
And one of our former students who is now
at the University of Utah,

00:06:34.063 --> 00:06:37.522
Chris Stanton, and I have been doing a
study looking at that specifically in

00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:40.570
the context of a firm where we can
actually measure productivity.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.370
And we do see people working harder during
the recession.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:47.340
Perhaps as a result of their being
somewhat more afraid that

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.280
they'll lose their jobs and

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.550
they want to protect their jobs and are
willing to work harder to do it.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:55.640
The question is whether that kind of
efficiency enhancement that occurs in

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.020
a short period of time can persist for a
long time,

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.600
and whether that can continue to grow and
undoubtful.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.690
So I think these increases in productivity
that we saw for

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:09.050
the short run, are not sustainable over
the long haul.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.090
In order to get productivity to grow over
the long haul,

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.200
we got to do a number of things.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.850
Most of it comes from the investment side.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:18.260
But it means investment in physical
capital, investment in human capital.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:19.236
That's what Stanford does.

00:07:19.236 --> 00:07:25.224
We are producers of human capital and, and
extremely good producers of human capital.

00:07:25.224 --> 00:07:28.546
And technology, which is derived from
those two.

00:07:28.546 --> 00:07:32.383
So I think that's the, that's the formula
for the long run.

00:07:32.383 --> 00:07:33.884
It's not making people work harder.

00:07:33.884 --> 00:07:39.056
[MUSIC]

00:07:39.056 --> 00:07:43.919
[MUSIC]

